package jpeg

import "core:testing"

// Fixtures below were generated with Pillow 10.2.0 (python3), quality
// 100 unless noted, subsampling=0 (forces 1x1 sampling on every
// component -- PIL leaves CMYK and grayscale images at 1x1 already,
// but RGB defaults to chroma subsampling without this). Every fixture
// was verified, via an independent from-scratch marker walk (not
// Pillow's own decoder), to carry a SOF0 (baseline) marker with every
// component's sampling factors equal to 1x1.
//
// PIL quirk: saving or loading a "CMYK" mode image inverts every
// sample byte (stores 255-value), unconditionally, regardless of any
// Adobe APP14 marker -- see JpegImagePlugin.py's RAWMODE table
// ("CMYK": "CMYK;I") and its unconditional use for both the save
// rawmode and the "assume adobe conventions" load rawmode. So for a
// CMYK fixture built from source channel values V, what is actually
// stored in the JPEG stream (what this decoder must return) is
// 255-V, not V. Concretely: an image was created with
// Image.new("CMYK", size, V), saved, then reopened with
// Image.open(...); the reopened pixel equalled V again (both
// inversions cancel on a Pillow round trip), and the expected raw
// values below are 255 minus that reopened value.
//
// For solid-color quality-100 images the forward DCT of a constant
// block is exact (only the DC term is non-zero) and quality 100
// quantization tables are all-1s (verified: DQT bytes are 0x01
// throughout), so the stored coefficients reproduce the source
// samples exactly; those tests assert exact equality. The gradient
// fixture carries real AC energy and was saved at quality 90, so it
// is checked within a +/-2 tolerance instead, referencing PIL's own
// decode (with the same inversion accounted for) as ground truth.

// 16x16 solid CMYK image, C=20 M=80 Y=150 K=200, quality 100.
// PIL reopen of pixel (0,0) is (20, 80, 150, 200) (round-trip
// identity, confirming quality-100 exactness); expected raw stored
